= Evidence — Technical =

== Ontology of evidence ==
Competing theories identify evidence with different kinds of entities:

* propositions;
* known facts;
* experiences;
* perceptual states;
* reasons;
* records or observations;
* publicly available data.

The choice affects whether two agents with the same external observations but different internal states possess the same evidence.

== Evidential support ==
Evidence can be modeled through several relations.

'''Deductive entailment''' — evidence logically guarantees a conclusion.

'''Probabilistic confirmation''' — evidence raises the probability or credence of a hypothesis.

A simple Bayesian support relation may compare:

<pre>
P(H | E) > P(H)
</pre>

but this criterion depends on prior probability and modeling assumptions.

'''Likelihood evidence''' compares how expected E is under competing hypotheses:

<pre>
P(E | H1) / P(E | H2)
</pre>

Likelihood ratios do not themselves produce posterior probabilities without further assumptions.

== Total evidence ==
The principle of total evidence says evidential assessment should use the relevant total body of evidence rather than selected favorable items.

New evidence can undercut:
* the truth of a premise;
* the reliability of a source;
* the relevance of a method;
* the independence of observations.

This gives rise to rebutting and undercutting defeaters.

== Dependence ==
Evidence aggregation requires a dependence model.

If E1 and E2 derive from the same data-generating process or source lineage, treating them as independent can double-count support.

Dependence can arise through:
* shared datasets;
* common instruments;
* copied claims;
* shared systematic errors;
* common priors/models;
* overlapping study populations.

[[Provenance/Technical|Provenance]] helps reveal these relations.

== Evidence and models ==
Scientific observations are often model-mediated.

Instrument output becomes evidential only through calibration, preprocessing, reconstruction, classification, statistical assumptions, or a [[Scientific model/Technical|scientific model]].

This does not imply that evidence is arbitrary. It means the inferential path must be explicit.

== Causal evidence ==
Causal inference requires assumptions beyond association.

Relevant technical tools include:
* randomized experiments;
* structural causal models;
* potential outcomes;
* instrumental variables;
* adjustment for confounding;
* natural experiments;
* mechanism evidence.

See [[Causality/Technical|Causality — Technical]].

